  "updated": "2026-08-10T06:44:22.181591+00:00",
  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1) \nMonday morning: The remnant cold pool air mass from the Sunday \nevening thunderstorms continues to modify into more unstable air. \nIncreasing southwesterly flow is resulting in rising temperatures \nacross portions of eastern Indiana and western Ohio within the last\nfew hours. If thunderstorms are able to form on the leading edge of\nthe low level jet, this recovery would present the opportunity for \nthunderstorms to organize and push southeastward into the local area\nlater this morning. \n\nRest of the day Monday: While any thunderstorm coverage during the\nmorning would complicate factors going into this afternoon and\nevening, the broader picture still supports an increased potential\nfor severe weather this afternoon and evening. Steady southwesterly \nflow continues to feed moisture northeastward resulting in plentiful \nCAPE across the boundary stretched from east to west from Iowa to \nsouthern Michigan. New thunderstorms will develop along this \nboundary, moving east and southeast throughout the late afternoon and\nevening. \n\nIn addition to the main thunderstorm period later today, given the\nlarge area hot and humid conditions, smaller clusters of \nthunderstorms may develop around the area. Limited some of the higher\ntemperature values in the NBM given the potential for thunderstorm\nactivity. \n\nThe low level jet remains strong into Monday night. Depending on the\namount of lingering instability, there is plenty of support for\nthunderstorms to develop into Tuesday morning. \n\nTuesday: Ongoing thunderstorms in the morning will complicate the \noverall forecast for Tuesday, however, a potent shortwave and \nstrengthening southwesterly low-level supports an active day\nregardless any early activity. Deep-layer shear is stronger Tuesday\nwhen compared to Monday, with forecast soundings indicating 0-6 km\nshear of 40-50 knots. If conditions align Tuesday afternoon and \nevening, an organized MCS is likely through central Indiana into much\nof the local area. Swaths of severe wind and spin-up tornadoes would\nbe likely as the line moves through the area. The exact placement and\ndegree of organization of the line is what remains the most \nuncertain. \n\nWhile an organized MCS would likely use much of the instability over\nthe area, much of the broader synoptic set up remains the same.\nContinued southwesterly flow within a strong low level jet will\nsupply more moisture northeastward. Any lingering outflow boundaries\ncould be the focus of thunderstorms overnight Tuesday into Wednesday\nmorning. By now, flash flooding may be the main concern with multiple\nrounds of thunderstorms. Elevated flash flooding concerns would\nlikely require a northwest to southeast orientated outflow boundary.\nThis would support repetitive rounds of thunderstorms over the same\nareas versus a progressive line that quickly moves through. \n\nWednesday: The boundary is likely far enough south that chances for\nsevere weather and flash flooding begin to decrease for the northern\nportions of the area. Another shortwave moves southeastward across\nthe area Wednesday afternoon and evening, supporting additional\nrounds of thunderstorms. Given that this event is still a couple days\naway, uncertainty is larger on the exact placement of the impacts.\nThere is even the possibility that much of the damaging wind and\nflash flooding threats would be outside the local area or at least\nisolated to the far southern portions of the area. \n\nRest of Period: Thursday, the mid-level ridge builds in a bit \nfarther to the north, presenting another opportunity for \nthunderstorms. On top of the rainfall from the previous days, \nflooding of area rivers could now become an increasing problem. A \nstrong enough trough progresses into the Northeast US to allow for\na much needed dry period Friday into Saturday. There are signs that\nadditional moisture may move back to the Ohio Valley, but the\nimmediate concern is with the events over the next couple of days.",
